## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic data structures with tight amortized bounds in a streaming context, including handling duplicates and edge cases like K=0. It also probes practical trade-offs for sliding windows.

## Key Concepts
- Maintain counts and a running pair counter
- Special-case K=0 to avoid double counting
- Support batched inserts/removals within a fixed window

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowPairCounter {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K; this.cnt = new Map(); this.pairs = 0;
  }
  add(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs += (this.cnt.get(x) || 0);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs += a + b;
    }
    this.cnt.set(x, (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1);
  }
  remove(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    const cur = this.cnt.get(x) || 0;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs -= (cur - 1);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs -= (a + b);
    }
    if (cur === 1) this.cnt.delete(x); else this.cnt.set(x, cur - 1);
  }
  batch(addArr, removeArr) {
    for (const x of removeArr) this.remove(x);
    for (const x of addArr) this.add(x);
  }
  exists() { return this.pairs > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if window size N changes over time?
- What are cache/memory implications when values are large or negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Analyzes streaming-frequency estimation with bounded memory and batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy hitters; counter trimming; candidate set.
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs memory; exact counting as baseline.
- Batch processing implications on update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Misra-Gries skeleton
class MG {
  constructor(K){ this.K=K; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){ if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1); else if(this.map.size<this.K) this.map.set(x,1); else { for(let [k,c] of this.map){ this.map.set(k,c-1); } /* remove zeros */ for(let [k,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(k)<=0) this.map.delete(k); } }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity-analysis: maintaining LIS under online batched updates tests block-based design, amortized reasoning, and practical trade-offs beyond typical path/graph problems.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) dynamics
- sqrt-decomposition / block partitioning
- per-block LIS profiles and cross-block composition
- amortized cost vs full recomputation
- practical optimizations and memory bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple block LIS recompute (tails-based)
function computeBlockLIS(block) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of block) {
    let i = 0, j = tails.length;
    while (i < j) {
      const m = (i + j) >> 1;
      if (tails[m] < x) i = m + 1; else j = m;
    }
    tails[i] = x;
  }
  return tails;
}

// Placeholder for merging block profiles into global LIS length
function mergeBlocks(blocks) {
  let tails = [];
  for (const b of blocks) {
    // apply b.profile to current tails (conceptual)
    tails = applyProfile(tails, b.profile);
  }
  return tails.length;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-increasing or multi-criteria LIS (e.g., by value and position)?
- What would be the impact of choosing K adaptively based on B and n?

